`@HttpMiddleware(handler)` attaches middleware to a resource and its nested
resources. Middleware runs in order from ancestor to child and can continue the
chain, short-circuit it, or wrap the downstream result.

## Write middleware

Middleware receives an `HttpContext` and an async `next` function. Use
`context.state` to share request-local data with later middleware or the
handler:

```ts
import {
  HttpContext,
  HttpGet,
  HttpMiddleware,
  HttpResource,
  HttpResult,
} from "@restfx/server";

async function requireToken(
  context: HttpContext,
  next: () => Promise<unknown>,
) {
  if (!context.req.headers.authorization) {
    return new HttpResult(401, { message: "Unauthorized" });
  }
  return next();
}

@HttpMiddleware(requireToken)
@HttpResource("private")
class PrivateResource {
  @HttpGet()
  getSecret() {
    return { secret: "available to authenticated callers" };
  }
}
```

Middleware can set response headers with `context.res.setHeader()`. The
request's `context.state` starts empty for each request; applications can
augment the exported `HttpContextState` interface to type custom properties.
Middleware exceptions follow the same server error handling as handler
exceptions.

## Configure CORS

Apply `@CorsMiddleware(options)` to the root resource to handle CORS headers and
preflight requests:

```ts
import { CorsMiddleware, HttpResource } from "@restfx/server";

@CorsMiddleware({
  origins: ["https://app.example.com", "*.preview.example.com"],
  credentials: true,
  allowedHeaders: ["content-type", "authorization"],
  exposedHeaders: ["x-total-count"],
  maxAge: 600,
})
@HttpResource()
class ApiResource {
  // Mount resources here.
}
```

`origins` accepts exact HTTP(S) origins and host patterns. A host-only wildcard
matches subdomains at any depth but not the apex domain, and allows either
scheme and any port. For example, `https://*.preview.example.com` restricts the
scheme; including a port restricts that too. Matching origins are echoed in
`Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, so credentialed requests work with an explicit
allowlist rather than `*`. Non-matching origins receive no CORS allow headers.

Preflight methods default to methods registered on the resource, including
implicit `HEAD` and `OPTIONS`; `methods` can override that list. If
`allowedHeaders` is omitted, requested preflight headers are reflected. The
middleware adds relevant `Vary` headers. CORS is a browser access policy, not an
authentication or authorization mechanism.

For cookie-based browser calls, pair `credentials: "include"` in the client
with `credentials: true` and an explicit allowed origin on the server. Browser
cookie `SameSite`, `Secure`, and domain rules still apply.

The `@restfx/core` package provides browser-safe model decorators, field types,
and runtime validation. The same model metadata can be used by server routes,
OpenAPI generation, and generated clients. Core has no Node.js or HTTP-server
dependency, so model classes can be imported by both browser and server code.

## Define a model

Decorate a class with `@Model()` and each accepted property with `@Field()`:

```ts
import { Email, Field, Model, StringField, validateModel } from "@restfx/core";

@Model()
class CreateUser {
  @Field({ type: Email })
  email!: string;

  @Field({ type: StringField, minLength: 2, maxLength: 80 })
  name!: string;

  @Field({ type: StringField, optional: true })
  nickname?: string;
}

const user = validateModel(
  { email: "ada@example.test", name: "Ada", ignored: true },
  CreateUser,
);
// `user` is a CreateUser instance; unknown fields are stripped by default.
```

Validation does not invoke the model constructor. It returns an instance
containing declared fields, with nested models validated recursively. Invalid
input throws `ModelValidationError`; its `issues` array contains `{ path,
message }` entries (for example, `address.city`). Models strip unknown fields by
default; use `@Model({ unknownFields: "reject" })` to reject them. The policy can
also be overridden for an individual call with
`validateModel(value, ModelClass, { unknownFields: "reject" })`.

## Field types and constraints

Built-in field types exported by core are:

- `StringField`, `BooleanField`, `NumberField`, and `IntegerField`.
- `Email` and `UUID`, which validate strings and emit schema formats.
- `DateField`, `TimeField`, and `DateTimeField`, which validate ISO/RFC 3339
  strings. Values remain strings; they are not converted to JavaScript `Date`s.
- `Enum([...])` for a fixed set of values.
- `ArrayOf(FieldType | Model)` for arrays.
- `UnionOf(...FieldTypes)` and `DiscriminatedUnion(key, variants)` for union
  values and decorated model variants.

`@Field` options describe presence, constraints, and schema documentation:

| Option                                  | Behavior                                                                                                         |
| --------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `optional`                              | Allows the property to be omitted.                                                                               |
| `nullable`                              | Allows explicit `null`; omission still depends on `optional`.                                                    |
| `default`                               | Used only when omitted. Object defaults are cloned; defaults are checked against the field type and constraints. |
| `minLength`, `maxLength`, `pattern`     | Constrain strings. `pattern` takes a `RegExp`.                                                                   |
| `minimum`, `maximum`                    | Constrain numbers.                                                                                               |
| `minItems`, `maxItems`, `uniqueItems`   | Constrain array size and uniqueness.                                                                             |
| `description`, `examples`, `deprecated` | Add documentation metadata to generated schemas.                                                                 |
| `schema`                                | Adds JSON Schema keywords, especially for custom field types.                                                    |

Constraints are checked at runtime and represented in generated JSON Schema and
OpenAPI where supported. Schema metadata does not perform runtime validation;
enforce application rules with a field type or model validator too.

## Nested models and inheritance

Use `model` to compose a decorated model as a nested object. Derived models
inherit decorated base fields and model options; a derived field with the same
name overrides the inherited field:

```ts
@Model()
class Address {
  @Field({ type: StringField })
  city!: string;
}

@Model({ unknownFields: "reject" })
class CreateProfile {
  @Field({ type: StringField })
  name!: string;

  @Field({ model: Address })
  address!: Address;
}

@Model()
class AdminProfile extends CreateProfile {
  @Field({ type: StringField })
  role!: string;
}
```

Nested values become model instances without calling constructors. Base and
derived model validators both run. Schema keywords merge across inheritance,
with derived keys taking precedence.

## Cross-field validation

Use a model-level `validate` callback when correctness depends on multiple
fields. It runs after field validation and may return no issue, one issue, or an
array of issues:

```ts
type DateRange = { start: number; end: number };

@Model<DateRange>({
  validate: ({ start, end }) =>
    start <= end
      ? undefined
      : { path: "end", message: "Must not precede start" },
})
class DateRangeModel {
  @Field({ type: NumberField })
  start!: number;

  @Field({ type: NumberField })
  end!: number;
}
```

## Request-source metadata

Core fields can declare `source: "path" | "query" | "header"` and an optional
`sourceName` for the external parameter name. A nested model can use
`source: "body"`. This metadata lets one model describe both the shape and
validation rules and where the server reads each value. Core itself does not
parse HTTP input; see [combined request models](./request-handling/).

## Typed configuration

`defineConfig()` preserves and type-checks an object against the small
`FrameworkConfig` shape, which currently contains an optional `port`:

```ts
import { defineConfig } from "@restfx/core";

export default defineConfig({ port: 3000 });
```

It does not load files, read environment variables, or start the server; the
application chooses how configuration is sourced and applied.

For the full reference, see the [`@restfx/core` README](https://github.com/thinkcraft/restfx/blob/main/packages/core/README.md).

For all field types, options, and model behaviors, see the
[`@restfx/core` README](https://github.com/thinkcraft/restfx/blob/main/packages/core/README.md).

`@restfx/server` generates an OpenAPI 3.1 document from registered routes,
decorator options, and `@restfx/core` model metadata. Add
`@OpenApiMiddleware(options)` to the root resource:

```ts
import { HttpResource, OpenApiMiddleware } from "@restfx/server";

@OpenApiMiddleware({
  path: "/openapi.json",
  info: {
    title: "Example API",
    version: "1.0.0",
    description: "Example service",
  },
  servers: [{ url: "https://api.example.com" }],
  securitySchemes: {
    bearerAuth: { type: "http", scheme: "bearer" },
  },
  security: [{ bearerAuth: [] }],
})
@HttpResource()
class ApiResource {
  // Mount route resources here.
}
```

The endpoint defaults to `/openapi.json`, serves `GET` and `HEAD`, answers
`OPTIONS`, and returns `405` for other methods. The document is generated on
the first `GET` or `HEAD` and cached. Root middleware runs before the
OpenAPI response. The configured path must be an absolute URL path and must not
overlap an application route.

## Add operation metadata

HTTP method decorators accept OpenAPI operation fields such as `summary`,
`description`, `operationId`, `tags`, `deprecated`, `parameters`, and `security`.
They also accept `requestBody`, `responses`, and the model shortcuts `body`,
`request`, and `response`:

```ts
@HttpGet({
  path: ":id",
  summary: "Get a user",
  operationId: "getUser",
  tags: ["users"],
  parameters: [
    { name: "include", in: "query", schema: { type: "boolean" } },
  ],
  responses: { "404": { description: "User not found" } },
})
getUser() {
  // ...
}
```

Use `@HttpResource({ path, tag, description })` to add a resource prefix and
describe its OpenAPI tag. Use `@Model({ description })` for model descriptions.
Operation IDs must be unique. By default, the method name is used when unique;
set `operationId` explicitly when method names are repeated or route variants
need stable names. Optional final route parameters generate both path variants.

Routes contribute methods and paths, inferred path parameters, model schemas,
and standard request-validation responses. Built-in types and field constraints
map to JSON Schema; semantic types like `UUID` and `Email` also emit their
formats. For non-model response bodies, document the response schema and content
type in `responses`. A custom field's `schema` metadata enriches the generated
schema but does not perform runtime validation.

## Serve a reference UI

Set `ui` to expose an interactive explorer alongside the JSON document:

```ts
@OpenApiMiddleware({
  path: "/openapi.json",
  ui: { type: "scalar", path: "/docs" },
  info: { title: "Example API", version: "1.0.0" },
})
@HttpResource()
class ApiResource {}
```

Choose `type: "scalar"`, `"swagger"`, or `"redoc"`; the UI path defaults to
`/docs` and must differ from the document path. The UI HTML loads its UI bundle
from a third-party CDN (Scalar from jsDelivr, Swagger UI from unpkg, or Redoc
from cdn.redoc.ly). Consider the external network and Content Security Policy
implications for your deployment. Omit `ui` to serve only the JSON document.

OpenAPI generation describes the HTTP contract; it does not replace runtime
validation or authentication. Security schemes and operation security describe
the contract only; they do not authenticate requests. See [model validation](./models/) and
[request handling](./request-handling/) for runtime behavior. The client
generator consumes this document; see [typed clients](./client/).

`HttpServer` wraps Node's `node:http` server and accepts framework options as
its second constructor argument. It can be used alongside Node HTTP server
configuration without moving transport-specific code into `@restfx/core`.

## Errors and observability

By default, `HttpError` responses preserve their status and details, while
unexpected errors produce a generic `500`. Set `errorHandler` to customize the
response. `onRequest`, `onError`, and `onResponse` hooks are useful for logging
and metrics; hook failures are isolated so they do not change request behavior:

```ts
const server = new HttpServer(ApiResource, {
  onError(error, context) {
    logger.error({ error, requestId: context?.requestId });
  },
  onResponse(context, metrics) {
    logger.info({
      requestId: context.requestId,
      path: context.req.path,
      statusCode: metrics.statusCode,
      durationMs: metrics.durationMs,
      completed: metrics.completed,
    });
  },
  errorHandler(error) {
    if (error instanceof HttpError && error.status === 404) {
      return new HttpResult(404, { message: "Not found" });
    }
    return new HttpResult(500, { message: "Request failed" });
  },
});
```

`errorHandler` is not called after response headers have been sent. Streaming
errors after data has started close the connection because a replacement error
response is no longer possible. `onResponse` reports status, duration, and
whether the response completed.

Each request gets a `context.requestId`. A valid incoming `X-Request-Id` token
(1–128 characters) is retained; otherwise the server creates a UUID. The ID is
returned in the response header and is available to hooks. Treat a client
supplied ID as a correlation value, never as identity or authorization data.

## Node server options and lifecycle

Pass `serverOptions` through to Node's `http.createServer()` (for example,
`headersTimeout` and `requestTimeout`). `listen(port = 3000)` returns the Node
`Server` instance. `await server.close()` stops accepting connections and waits
for active requests; `close({ timeoutMs })` force-closes remaining connections
after the timeout. Closing before listening is safe.

## Reverse proxies

Forwarded headers are ignored unless the connecting peer is trusted. Configure
`trustProxy` as a predicate over the remote socket address, and trust only the
addresses of your actual proxy:

```ts
const server = new HttpServer(ApiResource, {
  trustProxy: (remoteAddress) =>
    remoteAddress === "10.0.0.10" || remoteAddress === "::ffff:10.0.0.10",
});
```

For a trusted peer, the server uses validated singular `X-Forwarded-Host` and
`X-Forwarded-Proto` values (`http` or `https`). Configure proxies to overwrite
or sanitize forwarded headers. Do not trust arbitrary clients or broad address
ranges without an explicit network boundary.

## Configuration and examples

Core's `defineConfig()` is a typed helper for application configuration, not an
environment loader. Read environment variables and validate deployment
settings in your application, then pass the chosen port and server options to
`HttpServer`. The [playground](https://github.com/thinkcraft/restfx/tree/main/apps/playground)
shows this with structured logging and an injected service container.

Each handler receives an `HttpContext` with `req`, `res`, `cookies`, `container`,
`state`, and an abort `signal`. `context.req.url` is a `URL`; `path` excludes
the query string, and `params` contains decoded route parameters.

## Routes and parameters

`@HttpResource(path?)` marks a resource class. Resource-valued fields mount
nested resources, and `@HttpGet`, `@HttpPost`, `@HttpPut`, `@HttpPatch`, and
`@HttpDelete` register handlers. Use `@HttpMethod("METHOD", options)` for other
methods. A `path` on a route is relative to its resource:

```ts
@HttpResource("users")
class UsersResource {
  @HttpGet({ path: ":userId" })
  getUser(context: HttpContext) {
    return { id: context.req.params.userId };
  }
}
```

Route parameters use `:name` for one required segment, `:name?` for an optional
final segment, and `:name*` for zero or more trailing segments (captured as a
decoded string joined with `/`). Static routes take precedence. `HEAD` uses an
explicit handler or falls back to `GET` with its response body suppressed. An
unhandled `OPTIONS` request is answered with `204` and `Allow`; a known path with
an unsupported method returns `405`, and an unknown path returns `404`.

Handlers may return values directly (strings are sent as text, other values as
JSON) or return `new HttpResult(status, body, headers)` to specify status,
headers, or an empty body.

## Parse and validate bodies

`await context.req.parseBody()` parses a request body once and reuses the
resulting promise. The default body limit is 1 MiB; pass a byte limit or
`{ maxBytes }` to change it. Supported content types are JSON and `+json`,
URL-encoded forms, multipart forms, and `application/octet-stream` (a `Buffer`).
Form fields are strings; repeated names become arrays. Multipart files have
`{ filename, contentType, data }`, with `data` as a `Buffer`.

Missing or unsupported content types return `415`, malformed or interrupted
bodies return `400`, and bodies over the configured limit return `413`. If a
route declares a `body` model, the server validates it before invoking the
handler:

```ts
@HttpPost({ body: CreateUser, response: UserResponse })
async create(context: HttpContext) {
  const input = await context.req.parseBody<CreateUser>();
  return createUser(input);
}
```

Invalid model data returns `422` with field-path issues. A response model
validates and shapes JSON output, stripping undeclared fields; use
`response: { arrayOf: UserResponse }` for a list. Response models do not apply
to streams or raw responses.

## Combine path, query, header, and body values

For one validated request object, declare each source in `@Field` metadata.
Query, path, and header values arrive as strings and are converted for numeric
and boolean field types; `ArrayOf` supports repeated query parameters. A body is
a nested model:

```ts
@Model()
class CreateUserBody {
  @Field({ type: StringField, minLength: 2 })
  name!: string;
}

@Model()
class CreateUserRequest {
  @Field({ type: UUID, source: "path" })
  userId!: string;

  @Field({ type: BooleanField, optional: true, source: "query" })
  dryRun?: boolean;

  @Field({ type: UUID, optional: true, source: "header", sourceName: "x-client-id" })
  clientId?: string;

  @Field({ model: CreateUserBody, source: "body" })
  body!: CreateUserBody;
}

@HttpPost({ path: ":userId", request: CreateUserRequest })
async create(context: HttpContext) {
  const request = await context.req.model(CreateUserRequest);
  return request;
}
```

The router validates declared request models before the handler; calling
`context.req.model()` returns the cached model. Invalid path/query/header or
body fields return `422`, while body parsing retains its `400`, `413`, and `415`
errors. OpenAPI parameters and request-body schema are derived from this
metadata. The older server-only `@PathParam`, `@QueryParam`, `@HeaderParam`, and
`@Body` decorators are also available for compatibility.

## Cookies

`context.cookies.get(name)` reads a percent-decoded request cookie;
`getAll(name)` returns repeated values. `set()` appends a `Set-Cookie` header and
`clear()` expires a cookie:

```ts
context.cookies.set("session", sessionToken, {
  httpOnly: true,
  secure: true,
  sameSite: "lax",
  path: "/",
  maxAge: 3600,
});
```

Options include `maxAge` in seconds, `expires`, `domain`, `path`, `httpOnly`,
`secure`, and `sameSite`. When clearing, pass the same path and domain used to
set the cookie. `SameSite=None`, `__Secure-`, and `__Host-` enforce required
secure/scope settings. Duplicate names are ambiguous with `get()`; use
`getAll()`. Malformed cookie headers return `400` when read. Cookie transport is
provided, but session storage, token signing, and authentication policy are the
application's responsibility.

## Stream responses and large uploads

Return an async generator to stream strings or `Uint8Array` chunks with
backpressure, rather than buffering the entire response. Set a content type
explicitly; streams otherwise use `application/octet-stream`:

```ts
@HttpGet({ path: "events" })
async *events(context: HttpContext) {
  context.res.setHeader("content-type", "text/event-stream; charset=utf-8");
  yield "event: ready\ndata: connected\n\n";
}
```

For uploads, `context.req.streamBody({ maxBytes })` returns a one-shot Node
readable stream, defaulting to the same 1 MiB limit. Pipe it directly to storage
using `node:stream/promises` `pipeline()` to preserve backpressure, and pass
`context.signal` to cancel work when the client disconnects. The body can be
consumed only once, either buffered with `parseBody()` or streamed. Limits are
checked against both `Content-Length` and received bytes. Treat client filenames
and content types as untrusted; choose storage paths on the server. Multipart
parsing is buffered and is intended for smaller uploads.

The `@restfx/server` package owns Node.js HTTP behavior. Decorated resources
organize routes, while models from `@restfx/core` describe and validate inputs.

## Resources and handlers

Use `@HttpResource(path?)` to define a resource and HTTP method decorators to
register handlers. Resource-valued fields create nested routes:

```ts
import { HttpContext, HttpGet, HttpResource, HttpServer } from "@restfx/server";

@HttpResource("posts")
class PostsResource {
  @HttpGet()
  list() {
    return [{ id: "1", title: "Hello" }];
  }

  @HttpGet({ path: ":id" })
  get(context: HttpContext) {
    return { id: context.req.params.id };
  }
}

@HttpResource("api")
class ApiResource {
  readonly posts = new PostsResource();
}

const server = new HttpServer(ApiResource);
server.listen(3000);
```

This registers `GET /api/posts` and `GET /api/posts/:id`. Handlers can return
values directly, or use `HttpResult` to set a status, headers, and response body.

Decorated resource-valued fields are discovered and mounted automatically; use
`@HttpResource(path?)` on a field to override the child path. Children can be
provided as instances or constructors, and static or instance fields are
supported. For each matched request, RestFX creates a fresh instance of the
matched (leaf) resource with its no-argument constructor; state on a supplied
leaf instance is not copied. Ancestor resources are shared. Prefer injected
services for shared state and request-scoped state rather than storing request
data on an ancestor resource.

## Request validation

Declare a body model in the route options. The server parses and validates the
body before invoking the handler:

```ts
@HttpPost({ body: CreatePost })
async create(context: HttpContext) {
  const post = await context.req.parseBody<CreatePost>();
  return new HttpResult(201, post);
}
```

The server also supports request models assembled from path, query, header, and
body values. See [request handling](./request-handling/) for parsing formats,
limits, combined request models, cookies, and streaming.

## Middleware, services, and OpenAPI

Resources can use scoped middleware, and an `IocContainer` can provide
singleton or request-lifetime services. The server can generate OpenAPI from
routes and model metadata, with optional interactive documentation UIs. Follow
the guides for [dependency injection](./dependency-injection/),
[middleware and CORS](./middleware/), [OpenAPI](./openapi/), and
[operations](./operations/).

For all server options and behavior, see the
[`@restfx/server` README](https://github.com/thinkcraft/restfx/blob/main/packages/server/README.md).
